CAT = "caguardProcedures"; SEG = "PRO"
# Syllabus section 3: surveillance, patrol, access, crowd and traffic control, handover, drug effects.
Q = [
("What is the main advantage of a foot patrol?",
 "You can use all your senses", ["It covers large areas quickly", "It needs no physical fitness", "It keeps you out of sight"],
 "On foot you can see, hear, smell and touch what is around you, and you are highly visible. The trade-off is limited coverage and mobility compared with a vehicle."),
("What is a drawback of patrolling a site by vehicle?",
 "Engine noise can mask sounds", ["It is too slow for large sites", "It cannot carry any equipment", "It is invisible to the public"],
 "A vehicle covers ground quickly but restricts what you can see and the engine can hide sounds such as breaking glass. Foot patrols pick up those details."),
("Why should you vary the times and routes of your patrols?",
 "So patrols are not predictable", ["To cut fuel and vehicle wear", "To shorten each of your shifts", "So clients cannot audit them"],
 "A fixed pattern lets someone time an offence for when you are elsewhere. Varying routes and timing keeps the deterrent effect."),
("What is the main weakness of a fixed surveillance post?",
 "Blind spots out of view", ["It gives no deterrent effect", "It cannot control access", "It needs a vehicle to work"],
 "A fixed post is good for access control and deterrence, but it relies mainly on vision and leaves areas you cannot see. Patrols or cameras cover those gaps."),
("What should an access control log record for each person?",
 "Name, time in and out", ["Home address and age", "Vehicle colour only", "Their reason for leaving"],
 "Recording each person's name with entry and exit times shows who was on site and when. That record matters in an emergency headcount or an investigation."),
("A crowd is turning hostile and you are alone. What should you do first?",
 "Call for police and backup", ["Wade in and remove the leader", "Lock yourself in the office", "Order everyone to go home now"],
 "Before acting in crowd control, weigh the risk to life, your own safety and the backup available. When a crowd escalates, call police rather than confronting it alone."),
("What should you wear when directing traffic?",
 "A reflective vest", ["A dark uniform jacket", "A police-style peaked cap", "Plain clothes for safety"],
 "Drivers must be able to see you, so traffic control calls for high-visibility reflective vests or cuffs. Police-style caps are prohibited for guards anyway."),
("At the end of your shift, what is the correct handover?",
 "Brief the incoming guard", ["Leave when your time is up", "Lock the logbook in the office", "Email the client a summary"],
 "A shift handover updates the next guard on open incidents, keys, alarms and instructions. Leaving without a briefing leaves the site blind."),
("Why learn what is normal for your site during patrols?",
 "To spot what is out of place", ["To finish patrols more quickly", "To avoid writing so many notes", "To plan your breaks better"],
 "Situational awareness starts with a baseline: once you know what is typical, small anomalies stand out. That is how minor but critical details get noticed."),
("Someone has been loitering near a loading door for an hour. What is the best response?",
 "Observe and record details", ["Arrest them straight away", "Ignore them until they leave", "Tell them to leave the city"],
 "Loitering alone is not an offence that allows arrest. Observe, note a description and times, and report or approach politely according to site orders."),
("A person has pinpoint pupils, very slow breathing and will not wake. Which drug type is most likely?",
 "An opioid", ["A stimulant", "Cannabis", "A hallucinogen"],
 "Pinpoint pupils, slow or stopped breathing and unresponsiveness are classic signs of opioid overdose and a medical emergency. Stimulants usually cause dilated pupils and agitation."),
("Which signs most suggest someone is high on a stimulant such as cocaine?",
 "Agitation and dilated pupils", ["Pinpoint pupils and drowsiness", "Slurred speech and stumbling", "Slow, shallow breathing"],
 "Stimulants speed the body up: agitation, a fast pulse, sweating and dilated pupils. Slurred speech and stumbling point to a depressant such as alcohol."),
("Slurred speech, poor balance and slowed reactions are typical of which drug type?",
 "Depressants, like alcohol", ["Stimulants such as cocaine", "Hallucinogens such as LSD", "Inhalants used once only"],
 "Depressants, including alcohol, slow the brain, so speech, balance and judgment suffer. Stimulants cause the opposite: restlessness and speed."),
("Seeing or hearing things that are not there most suggests which drug type?",
 "A hallucinogen", ["An opioid", "A depressant", "A stimulant"],
 "Hallucinogens alter perception and can cause vivid hallucinations and panic. Keep calm, reduce stimulation and get medical help if the person is at risk."),
("You find a used syringe in a stairwell. What should you do?",
 "Keep clear; use sharps procedure", ["Recap it, then put it in the trash", "Wrap it in paper and bin it", "Leave it and log it at shift end"],
 "Keep people away and dispose of it with the site's sharps procedure, using tools and a puncture-proof container. Recapping is a common cause of needlestick injuries."),
("A patron refuses a bag check at a venue entrance. What can you do?",
 "Refuse them entry", ["Search the bag by force", "Detain them for police", "Confiscate the bag"],
 "A bag check at entry is a condition of entry agreed to by consent. If they refuse, you can deny entry, but you have no power to force a search."),
("Which sense does CCTV surveillance rely on most?",
 "Sight", ["Hearing", "Smell", "Touch"],
 "Camera surveillance, like a fixed post, depends mainly on vision. That is why patrols that use every sense still matter."),
("Which type of intrusion alarm sensor is most common?",
 "Passive infrared", ["Seismic vibration", "Microwave", "Trip wire"],
 "Passive infrared sensors, which detect body heat moving across their field, are the most common. Microwave, ultrasonic and trip wire systems are less common or used alongside them."),
("How do you signal a driver to stop when directing traffic?",
 "Raise a flat palm", ["Wave your arm in a circle", "Point a finger at the driver", "Flash your flashlight twice"],
 "A raised arm with the palm facing the driver is the standard stop signal. Use clear, deliberate signals and make eye contact before stepping in front of a vehicle."),
("On patrol you find a door unlocked that is always locked. What should you do?",
 "Report it; follow site orders", ["Lock it and leave it out of notes", "Leave it for the day shift to fix", "Prop it open for your next patrol"],
 "An unexpected open door may mean a break-in, so report it and follow the post orders, which may call for backup before checking inside. Always record it in your notes."),
]
